## Provision the Compute Nodes

Everything the compute nodes need was staged on the head node during the
provisioner chapter: the install image, the OpenHPC package list
(`otherpkglist`/`otherpkgdir`), and the `ohpc-config` postscript. Provisioning
now applies all of it in a single pass -- associate the install image with the
*compute* group and boot the nodes, and xCAT installs the base OS onto local
disk, installs the OpenHPC packages from the staged repositories, runs the
configuration postscript, and reboots each node into a ready state.

The OpenHPC packages are pulled from the OpenHPC, EPEL, and CRB repositories
named by `otherpkgdir`, so the nodes must be able to reach them during the
install. By default xCAT points the compute default route at the head node;
ensure this provides a path to those repositories (for example via NAT on the
head node or a site gateway registered in the xCAT `networks` table), or host a
local mirror on the head node and name it in `otherpkgdir` instead.

The `${OHPC_POSTSCRIPT}` configuration script is now complete: the provisioner
chapter created it and the customization chapters appended to it. Review it
before provisioning -- it is a plain shell script, and this is the point to
confirm it holds what you expect (its commands are appended in order and are not
de-duplicated, so if you re-ran earlier steps, check for repeated lines):

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Review the assembled compute configuration postscript -->
<!-- ohpc_command cat "${OHPC_POSTSCRIPT}" -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Associate the install image with the compute group -->
```bash
# Associate provisioning image for compute group
nodeset compute osimage=${xcat_osimage}
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

{% include "provisioner/xcat/power-cycle.md.j2" %}

Continue after every compute node reports the `installing` status, which xCAT
sets when the installer starts. This also tells an installed node apart from
the system it ran before a reinstall, which still reports `booted` until then.
To check:

```bash
lsdef compute -i status -c
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait up to install_start_timeout for every compute node to report installing -->
<!-- ohpc_command deadline=$(( SECONDS + ${install_start_timeout:-600} )) -->
<!-- ohpc_command until [ "$(lsdef compute -i status -c | grep -c "=installing$")" -eq "$num_computes" ]; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     if (( SECONDS >= deadline )); then -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "installer: FAILED, not every node installing in ${install_start_timeout:-600}s"; lsdef compute -i status -c -->
<!-- ohpc_command         exit 1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 10 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

`rsetboot compute net` applies to the next boot only, and the boot into the
installer uses it up. When the installation finishes, a node whose firmware
prefers the network would boot from the network again instead of starting the
new system, so set the next boot to the local disk:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set has_ipmi -->
```bash
rsetboot compute hd
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Without a BMC, the site's reset handler must also leave the nodes booting from
their disk.

The stateful installation typically completes in 5 to 10 minutes, depending on
BIOS post times and disk speed. Monitor provisioning with the `rcons` command,
which displays the serial console for a selected node; the escape sequence to
exit `rcons` is `Ctrl-e c .` typed sequentially. Deployment progress can also
be queried with `nodestat compute`, which reports the installation state of
each node until an `sshd` response indicates the node is up.

Continue after every compute node reports the `booted` status, which xCAT sets
once a node has rebooted into its installed system and run its postscripts.
To check one node:

```bash
lsdef ${compute_prefix}1 -i status -c
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait up to install_timeout for every compute node to report booted -->
<!-- ohpc_command deadline=$(( SECONDS + ${install_timeout:-1800} )) -->
<!-- ohpc_command until [ "$(lsdef compute -i status -c | grep -c "=booted$")" -eq "$num_computes" ]; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     if (( SECONDS >= deadline )); then -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "stateful install: FAILED, not booted in ${install_timeout:-1800}s"; lsdef compute -i status -c -->
<!-- ohpc_command         exit 1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "Waiting for the stateful install..." -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 30 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

{% include "boot/wait.md.j2" %}

Once installed, verify access with a parallel command across the compute nodes
using `xdsh`, xCAT's native distributed shell:

```bash
xdsh compute uptime
c1:  12:56:50 up  4 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.01, 0.04
c2:  12:56:50 up  3 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.02, 0.05
```
